### Using the information to solve

In order to find $a'(t_0)$ we need to find $a(t_0)$. Using the Pythagorean theorem and the fact that $b(t_0)=36$ and $c=39$, we can find that $a(t_0)=15$.

Let's plug $\blueD{b(t_0)}=\blueD{36}$, $\maroonD{b'(t_0)}=\maroonD{10}$, and $\goldD{a(t_0)}=\goldD{15}$ into the expression for $a'(t_0)$:

$\begin{align}
a'(t_0)&=-\dfrac{\blueD{b(t_0)}\maroonD{b'(t_0)}}{\goldD{a(t_0)}}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{(\blueD{36})(\maroonD{10})}{(\goldD{15})}
\\\\
&=-24
\end{align}$
